diagnosis_text:
I verified both parts of the user’s suspicion: R12’s LAN interface to PC12 and R12’s EIGRP adjacency upstream. The LAN gateway 120.1.1.1 (r12 eth1 on segment GG) is stable. From PC12 I ran high‑rate probes to the gateway (50 pings at 20 Hz and an additional burst) with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTTs. PC12’s ARP for 120.1.1.1 is consistently REACHABLE, and R12 shows the client 120.1.1.10 in neighbor tables (transient NUD DELAY is normal under light traffic). On R12, eth1 reports link detected: yes, no carrier events, and interface counters show zero errors/drops. Kernel logs contain no link down/up messages for eth1.

Upstream routing is also steady. R12’s FRR EIGRP neighbor on eth0 (112.1.1.1) is “Up,” and /var/log/frr/frr.log shows a single adjacency establishment after service start with no subsequent resets. R11 corroborates stable EIGRP neighbors (including to R12) with no flapping in its log.

Mechanistically, if the default gateway or EIGRP session were flapping, we would observe ICMP loss from PC12 to 120.1.1.1, ARP resolution failures, carrier down/up messages, or EIGRP neighbor resets in the logs. None of these are present. Localization: R12 eth1 (GG) to PC12 and R12 eth0 (EE) to R11 are healthy.

Root cause: No network-side fault found; symptoms are not reproduced.

Corrective fix: No changes required on R12. Recommend the user monitor their host/app and, if issues persist, capture a timestamped ping/trace from PC12 to 120.1.1.1 and upstream targets to correlate with application timeouts.
=== END ===